<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · date unknown · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Bhaja Govindam</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="sa">भज गोविन्दम्</bdi> / <bdi lang="sa-Latn">Bhaja Govindam</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Sanskrit hymn said to be by the teacher Adi Shankara, telling people to praise God instead of wasting their lives on things that pass.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Adi Shankara (traditional attribution; fourteen further verses attributed to his disciples)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Hindu devotees: Smarthas, Vaishnavas and others</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Varanasi, India (in the traditional story)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditionally attributed to Adi Shankara; date not known</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditionally attributed to Adi Shankara; a medieval Sanskrit hymn)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaja-govindam">Bhaja Govindam</a>&quot; (&quot;praise Govinda&quot;, or &quot;seek Govinda&quot;), also called &quot;Moha Mudgara&quot; (&quot;destroyer of illusion&quot;), is a popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotional poem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> traditionally attributed to Adi Shankara. Its opening stanza is a refrain repeated after each verse, as Swami Chinmayananda describes it. The twelve verses after it, the Dvadasa-manjarika-stotram, are traditionally Shankara's own; fourteen more, the Caturdasa-manjarika-stotram, are attributed to fourteen of his disciples. Editions differ in the number, order, wording and arrangement of the stanzas, but not in their meaning. The poem is written in a moraic metre, measured in syllabic instants (mātrās) rather than a fixed number of syllables: apart from the first verse, every verse has sixteen mātrās, the padakulakam of the metrical handbook Vrtta-ratnakara.</p><p>A traditional story tells how it came to be. Walking with his disciples along a street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, Shankara met an old scholar reciting the rules of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> grammar over and over. Taking pity on him, he advised the man not to spend his last years on grammar but to turn his mind to God in worship, which alone could free him from the cycle of birth and death; the hymn is said to have been composed then. It shows that Shankara, often regarded as the reviver of the path of knowledge (jnana marga) to liberation, also taught the path of devotion (bhakti marga), the emphasis of the bhakti movement. In his commentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-rajagopalachari">C. Rajagopalachari</a> wrote that knowledge which has matured is spoken of as devotion, and that knowledge which does not become devotion is &quot;useless tinsel&quot;. Many scholars hold that the poem sums up, briefly and simply, the substance of Shankara's Vedantic thought, and because its refrain invokes God as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> it is sung not only by Shankara's own followers, the Smarthas, but also by Vaishnavas and others.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to turn from worldly learning and the ego towards devotion to God and surrender to him, which alone frees a person from the cycle of birth and death; urgent in tone, brought back again and again by its refrain.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Sanskrit</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Sanskrit</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">भजगोविन्दं भजगोविन्दं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bhajagovindaṃ bhajagovindaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Worship Govinda, worship Govinda,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">गोविन्दं भजमूढमते ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">govindaṃ bhajamūḍhamate |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>worship Govinda, O fool.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">संप्राप्ते सन्निहिते काले</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">saṃprāpte sannihite kāle</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the appointed time has come and is near,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नहि नहि रक्षति डुकृञ्करणे ॥ १॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nahi nahi rakṣati ḍukṛñkaraṇe ||1||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>'ḍukṛñ karaṇe' will not, will not save you. (1)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मूढ जहीहि धनागमतृष्णां</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">mūḍha jahīhi dhanāgamatṛṣṇāṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fool, give up the thirst for gaining wealth;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कुरु सद्बुद्धिं मनसि वितृष्णाम्।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kuru sadbuddhiṃ manasi vitṛṣṇām |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make your understanding good, and your mind free of thirst.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">यल्लभसे निजकर्मोपात्तं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yallabhase nijakarmopāttaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whatever wealth you gain, earned by your own work,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वित्तं तेन विनोदय चित्तम् ॥ २॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vittaṃ tena vinodaya cittam ||2||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with that content your mind. (2)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नारीस्तनभर नाभीदेशं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nārīstanabhara nābhīdeśaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Seeing the weight of a woman's breasts and the region of her navel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">दृष्ट्वा मागामोहावेशम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dṛṣṭvā māgāmohāveśam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do not fall into the grip of delusion.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">एतन्मांसावसादि विकारं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">etanmāṃsāvasādi vikāraṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This is only a change of flesh, fat and the like:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मनसि विचिन्तय वारं वारम् ॥ ३॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">manasi vicintaya vāraṃ vāram ||3||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>think on it in your mind again and again. (3)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नलिनीदलगत जलमतितरलं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nalinīdalagata jalamatitaralaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Water lying on a lotus leaf is very unsteady;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तद्वज्जीवितमतिशयचपलम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tadvajjīvitamatiśayacapalam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>life is like it, exceedingly fickle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">विद्धि व्याध्यभिमानग्रस्तं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">viddhi vyādhyabhimānagrastaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Know that the world is seized by sickness and conceit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">लोकं शोकहतं च समस्तम् ॥ ४॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">lokaṃ śokahataṃ ca samastam ||4||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and that all of it is struck down by grief. (4)</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 136 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">यावद्वित्तोपार्जन सक्तः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yāvadvittopārjana saktaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As long as you are bent on earning wealth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">स्तावन्निज परिवारो रक्तः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">stāvannija parivāro raktaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so long is your own family fond of you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पश्चाज्जीवति जर्जर देहे</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">paścājjīvati jarjara dehe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Afterwards, when you live on in a worn-out body,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वार्तां कोऽपि न पृच्छति गेहे ॥ ५॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vārtāṃ ko'pi na pṛcchati gehe ||5||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no one at home asks so much as a word of you. (5)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">यावत्पवनो निवसति देहे</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yāvatpavano nivasati dehe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As long as the breath dwells in the body,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तावत्पृच्छति कुशलं गेहे ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tāvatpṛcchati kuśalaṃ gehe |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so long they ask after your welfare at home.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">गतवति वायौ देहापाये</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">gatavati vāyau dehāpāye</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the breath has gone and the body has perished,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">भार्या बिभ्यति तस्मिन्काये ॥ ६॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bhāryā bibhyati tasminkāye ||6||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your wife is afraid of that body. (6)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">बालस्तावत्क्रीडासक्तः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bālastāvatkrīḍāsaktaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While a child, one is attached to play;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तरुणस्तावत्तरुणीसक्तः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">taruṇastāvattaruṇīsaktaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>while a youth, one is attached to young women;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वृद्धस्तावच्चिन्तासक्तः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vṛddhastāvaccintāsaktaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>while old, one is attached to worry;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">परे ब्रह्मणि कोऽपि न सक्तः ॥ ७॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">pare brahmaṇi ko'pi na saktaḥ ||7||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no one is attached to the supreme Brahman. (7)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">काते कान्ता कस्ते पुत्रः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kāte kāntā kaste putraḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who is your beloved? Who is your son?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">संसारोऽयमतीव विचित्रः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">saṃsāro'yamatīva vicitraḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This worldly round is very strange.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कस्य त्वं कः कुत आयातः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kasya tvaṃ kaḥ kuta āyātaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whose are you? Who are you? From where have you come?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तत्त्वं चिन्तय तदिह भ्रातः ॥ ८॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tattvaṃ cintaya tadiha bhrātaḥ ||8||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Think on the truth of this, here, brother. (8)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सत्सङ्गत्वे निस्सङ्गत्वं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">satsaṅgatve nissaṅgatvaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the company of the good, freedom from attachment;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">निस्सङ्गत्वे निर्मोहत्वम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nissaṅgatve nirmohatvam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from freedom from attachment, freedom from delusion;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">निर्मोहत्वे निश्चलतत्त्वं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nirmohatve niścalatattvaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from freedom from delusion, the unmoving truth;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">निश्चलतत्त्वे जीवन्मुक्तिः ॥ ९॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">niścalatattve jīvanmuktiḥ ||9||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the unmoving truth, liberation while still living. (9)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वयसिगते कः कामविकारः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vayasigate kaḥ kāmavikāraḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When youth has gone, what stirring of desire?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">शुष्के नीरे कः कासारः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śuṣke nīre kaḥ kāsāraḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the water has dried up, what lake?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">क्षीणेवित्ते कः परिवारः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kṣīṇevitte kaḥ parivāraḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When wealth has dwindled, what family?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">ज्ञाते तत्त्वे कः संसारः ॥ १०॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">jñāte tattve kaḥ saṃsāraḥ ||10||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the truth is known, what worldly round? (10)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मा कुरु धन जन यौवन गर्वं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">mā kuru dhana jana yauvana garvaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Take no pride in wealth, in people, in youth:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">हरति निमेषात्कालः सर्वम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">harati nimeṣātkālaḥ sarvam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>time takes all of it away in the blink of an eye.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मायामयमिदमखिलं हित्वा</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">māyāmayamidamakhilaṃ hitvā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Leaving all this, which is made of illusion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">ब्रह्मपदं त्वं प्रविश विदित्वा ॥ ११॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">brahmapadaṃ tvaṃ praviśa viditvā ||11||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>know the state of Brahman and enter it. (11)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">दिनयामिन्यौ सायं प्रातः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dinayāminyau sāyaṃ prātaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Day and night, evening and morning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">शिशिरवसन्तौ पुनरायातः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śiśiravasantau punarāyātaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>winter and spring come back again.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कालः क्रीडति गच्छत्यायुः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kālaḥ krīḍati gacchatyāyuḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Time plays, life goes by,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तदपि न मुञ्चत्याशावायुः ॥ १२॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tadapi na muñcatyāśāvāyuḥ ||12||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and still the wind of hope does not let go. (12)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">द्वादशमञ्जरिकाभिरशेषः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dvādaśamañjarikābhiraśeṣaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this cluster of twelve verses, in full,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कथितो वैयाकरणस्यैषः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kathito vaiyākaraṇasyaiṣaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>this was told to the grammarian:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">उपदेशो भूद्विद्यानिपुणैः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">upadeśo bhūdvidyānipuṇaiḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the teaching was given by the one skilled in learning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">श्रीमच्छन्करभगवच्छरणैः ॥ १२अ ॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śrīmacchankarabhagavaccharaṇaiḥ ||12a||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the revered Lord Shankara. (12a)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">काते कान्ता धन गतचिन्ता</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kāte kāntā dhana gatacintā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What is this worry of yours about your beloved and your wealth?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वातुल किं तव नास्ति नियन्ता ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vātula kiṃ tava nāsti niyantā |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Madman, have you no one to govern you?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">त्रिजगति सज्जनसं गतिरेका</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">trijagati sajjanasaṃ gatirekā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the three worlds the company of the good, and it alone,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">भवति भवार्णवतरणे नौका ॥ १३॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bhavati bhavārṇavataraṇe naukā ||13||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>becomes the boat for crossing the ocean of worldly existence. (13)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">जटिलो मुण्डी लुञ्छितकेशः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">jaṭilo muṇḍī luñchitakeśaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One with matted locks, one shaven, one with his hair plucked out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">काषायाम्बरबहुकृतवेषः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kāṣāyāmbarabahukṛtaveṣaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>one who has put on many guises in ochre robes:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पश्यन्नपि चन पश्यति मूढः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">paśyannapi cana paśyati mūḍhaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though he sees, the fool does not see;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">उदरनिमित्तं बहुकृतवेषः ॥ १४॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">udaranimittaṃ bahukṛtaveṣaḥ ||14||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for his belly's sake he has put on many guises. (14)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">अङ्गं गलितं पलितं मुण्डं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">aṅgaṃ galitaṃ palitaṃ muṇḍaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The limbs have grown weak, the head has turned grey,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">दशनविहीनं जातं तुण्डम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">daśanavihīnaṃ jātaṃ tuṇḍam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the mouth has become toothless;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वृद्धो याति गृहीत्वा दण्डं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vṛddho yāti gṛhītvā daṇḍaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the old man goes about holding a stick,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तदपि न मुञ्चत्याशापिण्डम् ॥ १५॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tadapi na muñcatyāśāpiṇḍam ||15||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and still he does not let go of the lump of hope. (15)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">अग्रे वह्निः पृष्ठेभानुः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">agre vahniḥ pṛṣṭhebhānuḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fire in front, the sun behind;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">रात्रौ चुबुकसमर्पितजानुः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">rātrau cubukasamarpitajānuḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at night, the knees drawn up to the chin;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">करतलभिक्षस्तरुतलवासः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">karatalabhikṣastarutalavāsaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alms in the palm of the hand, a dwelling at the foot of a tree:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तदपि न मुञ्चत्याशापाशः ॥ १६॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tadapi na muñcatyāśāpāśaḥ ||16||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and still the noose of hope does not let him go. (16)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कुरुते गङ्गासागरगमनं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kurute gaṅgāsāgaragamanaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One may make the journey to Gangasagar,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">व्रतपरिपालनमथवा दानम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vrataparipālanamathavā dānam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>keep vows, or give gifts;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">ज्ञानविहीनः सर्वमतेन</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">jñānavihīnaḥ sarvamatena</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>without knowledge, in the view of all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मुक्तिं न भजति जन्मशतेन ॥ १७॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">muktiṃ na bhajati janmaśatena ||17||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>one does not attain liberation even in a hundred births. (17)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सुर मंदिर तरु मूल निवासः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sura maṃdira taru mūla nivāsaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Dwelling in temples of the gods or at the foot of trees,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">शय्या भूतल मजिनं वासः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śayyā bhūtala majinaṃ vāsaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the bare ground for a bed, a deer-skin for clothing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सर्व परिग्रह भोग त्यागः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sarva parigraha bhoga tyāgaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>giving up all possessions and enjoyment:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कस्य सुखं न करोति विरागः ॥ १८॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kasya sukhaṃ na karoti virāgaḥ ||18||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whom does such detachment not make happy? (18)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">योगरतो वाभोगरतोवा</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yogarato vābhogaratovā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whether delighting in yoga or delighting in pleasure,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सङ्गरतो वा सङ्गविहीनः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">saṅgarato vā saṅgavihīnaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whether delighting in company or without company,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">यस्य ब्रह्मणि रमते चित्तं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yasya brahmaṇi ramate cittaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he whose mind delights in Brahman</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नन्दति नन्दति नन्दत्येव ॥ १९॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nandati nandati nandatyeva ||19||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rejoices, rejoices, simply rejoices. (19)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">भगवद् गीता किञ्चिदधीता</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bhagavad gītā kiñcidadhītā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One by whom the Bhagavad Gita has been studied even a little,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">गङ्गा जललव कणिकापीता ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">gaṅgā jalalava kaṇikāpītā |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by whom a drop of the water of the Ganges has been drunk,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सकृदपि येन मुरारि समर्चा</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sakṛdapi yena murāri samarcā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by whom the worship of Murari is performed even once:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">क्रियते तस्य यमेन न चर्चा ॥ २०॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kriyate tasya yamena na carcā ||20||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with him Yama has no dispute. (20)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पुनरपि जननं पुनरपि मरणं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">punarapi jananaṃ punarapi maraṇaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Birth again, death again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पुनरपि जननी जठरे शयनम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">punarapi jananī jaṭhare śayanam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lying again in a mother's womb:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">इह संसारे बहुदुस्तारे</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">iha saṃsāre bahudustāre</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>here in this worldly round, so hard to cross,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कृपयाऽपारे पाहि मुरारे ॥ २१॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kṛpayā'pāre pāhi murāre ||21||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>shoreless, save me by your grace, O Murari. (21)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">रथ्या चर्पट विरचित कन्थः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">rathyā carpaṭa viracita kanthaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His patched cloak made of rags from the street,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पुण्यापुण्य विवर्जित पन्थः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">puṇyāpuṇya vivarjita panthaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>his path free of merit and of sin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">योगी योगनियोजित चित्तो</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yogī yoganiyojita citto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the yogi, his mind fixed in yoga,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">रमते बालोन्मत्तवदेव ॥ २२॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">ramate bālonmattavadeva ||22||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>delights just like a child or a madman. (22)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कस्त्वं कोऽहं कुत आयातः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kastvaṃ ko'haṃ kuta āyātaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who are you? Who am I? From where has one come?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">का मे जननी को मे तातः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kā me jananī ko me tātaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who is my mother? Who is my father?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">इति परिभावय सर्वमसारम्</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">iti paribhāvaya sarvamasāram</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Consider all this to be without substance,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">विश्वं त्यक्त्वा स्वप्न विचारम् ॥ २३॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">viśvaṃ tyaktvā svapna vicāram ||23||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and leave the world, an imagining of dreams. (23)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">त्वयि मयि चान्यत्रैको विष्णुः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tvayi mayi cānyatraiko viṣṇuḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In you, in me and elsewhere there is one Vishnu;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">व्यर्थं कुप्यसि मय्यसहिष्णुः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vyarthaṃ kupyasi mayyasahiṣṇuḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in vain you are angry with me, impatient.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">भव समचित्तः सर्वत्र त्वं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bhava samacittaḥ sarvatra tvaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Be even-minded everywhere,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">वाञ्छस्यचिराद्यदि विष्णुत्वम् ॥ २४॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">vāñchasyacirādyadi viṣṇutvam ||24||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if you wish soon for the state of Vishnu. (24)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">शत्रौ मित्रे पुत्रे बन्धौ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śatrau mitre putre bandhau</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With enemy, friend, son or kinsman</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मा कुरु यत्नं विग्रहसन्धौ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">mā kuru yatnaṃ vigrahasandhau |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make no effort at quarrel or at peace.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सर्वस्मिन्नपि पश्यात्मानं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sarvasminnapi paśyātmānaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See the Self in everything;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सर्वत्रोत्सृज भेदाज्ञानम् ॥ २५॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sarvatrotsṛja bhedājñānam ||25||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>everywhere cast off the ignorance that sees difference. (25)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कामं क्रोधं लोभं मोहं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kāmaṃ krodhaṃ lobhaṃ mohaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Leaving desire, anger, greed and delusion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">त्यक्त्वाऽत्मानं भावय कोऽहम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tyaktvā'tmānaṃ bhāvaya ko'ham |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reflect on the Self: 'Who am I?'</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">आत्मज्ञान विहीना मूढाः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">ātmajñāna vihīnā mūḍhāḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fools without knowledge of the Self</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">ते पच्यन्ते नरकनिगूढाः ॥ २६॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">te pacyante narakanigūḍhāḥ ||26||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are cooked, hidden away in hell. (26)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">गेयं गीता नाम सहस्रं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">geyaṃ gītā nāma sahasraṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Gita and the Thousand Names are to be sung;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">ध्येयं श्रीपति रूपमजस्रम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">dhyeyaṃ śrīpati rūpamajasram |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the form of the Lord of Shri is to be meditated on without end;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नेयं सज्जन सङ्गे चित्तं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">neyaṃ sajjana saṅge cittaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the mind is to be led into the company of the good;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">देयं दीनजनाय च वित्तम् ॥ २७॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">deyaṃ dīnajanāya ca vittam ||27||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and wealth is to be given to the poor. (27)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सुखतः क्रियते रामाभोगः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sukhataḥ kriyate rāmābhogaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For pleasure one takes one's enjoyment of women;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पश्चाद्धन्त शरीरे रोगः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">paścāddhanta śarīre rogaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>afterwards, alas, disease in the body.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">यद्यपि लोके मरणं शरणं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">yadyapi loke maraṇaṃ śaraṇaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though in the world death is the last refuge,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">तदपि न मुञ्चति पापाचरणम् ॥ २८॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">tadapi na muñcati pāpācaraṇam ||28||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>still one does not give up sinful conduct. (28)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">अर्थमनर्थं भावय नित्यं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">arthamanarthaṃ bhāvaya nityaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Always consider wealth to be ruin:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नास्तिततः सुखलेशः सत्यम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nāstitataḥ sukhaleśaḥ satyam |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>truly, there is not a trace of happiness from it.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">पुत्रादपि धन भाजां भीतिः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">putrādapi dhana bhājāṃ bhītiḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Those who have wealth fear even their own sons:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सर्वत्रैषा विहिता रीतिः ॥ २९॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sarvatraiṣā vihitā rītiḥ ||29||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>everywhere this is the established way. (29)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">प्राणायामं प्रत्याहारं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">prāṇāyāmaṃ pratyāhāraṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Control of the breath, withdrawal of the senses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नित्यानित्य विवेकविचारम् ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nityānitya vivekavicāram |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reflection that tells the lasting from the fleeting,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">जाप्यसमेत समाधिविधानं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">jāpyasameta samādhividhānaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the practice of meditative absorption joined with recitation:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">कुर्ववधानं महदवधानम् ॥ ३०॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">kurvavadhānaṃ mahadavadhānam ||30||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>do these with care, with great care. (30)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">गुरुचरणाम्बुज निर्भर भक्तः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">gurucaraṇāmbuja nirbhara bhaktaḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wholly devoted to the lotus feet of the guru,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">संसारादचिराद्भव मुक्तः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">saṃsārādacirādbhava muktaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>be free soon of the worldly round.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">सेन्द्रियमानस नियमादेवं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">sendriyamānasa niyamādevaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thus, by restraint of the senses and the mind,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">द्रक्ष्यसि निज हृदयस्थं देवम् ॥ ३१॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">drakṣyasi nija hṛdayasthaṃ devam ||31||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you will see the god who dwells in your own heart. (31)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">मूढः कश्चन वैयाकरणो</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">mūḍhaḥ kaścana vaiyākaraṇo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A certain foolish grammarian,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">डुकृञ्करणाध्ययन धुरीणः ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">ḍukṛñkaraṇādhyayana dhurīṇaḥ |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>foremost in the study of 'ḍukṛñ karaṇe',</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">श्रीमच्छम्कर भगवच्छिष्यैः</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">śrīmacchamkara bhagavacchiṣyaiḥ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>was taught by the disciples of the revered Lord Shankara</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">बोधित आसीच्छोधितकरणः ॥ ३२॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bodhita āsīcchodhitakaraṇaḥ ||32||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and had his faculties made pure. (32)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">भजगोविन्दं भजगोविन्दं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">bhajagovindaṃ bhajagovindaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Worship Govinda, worship Govinda,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">गोविन्दं भजमूढमते ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">govindaṃ bhajamūḍhamate |</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>worship Govinda, O fool.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नामस्मरणादन्यमुपायं</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nāmasmaraṇādanyamupāyaṃ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Other than the remembering of his name, any means</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="sa">नहि पश्यामो भवतरणे ॥ ३३॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="sa-Latn">nahi paśyāmo bhavataraṇe ||33||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we do not see for crossing over worldly existence. (33)</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: traditional text (Sanskrit Wikisource). Text from <a href="https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A4%AD%E0%A4%9C%E0%A4%97%E0%A5%8B%E0%A4%B5%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%A8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%A6%E0%A4%AE%E0%A5%8D" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (IAST).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (36)</summary><ul><li>Line references are stanza.line in the order of the text printed here: stanza 13 is the edition's verse 12a, so from stanza 14 on, stanza n is verse n-1. The verse numbers are kept at the ends of the verses, as (1) in the English and ||1|| in the romanization; the edition's १२अ is 12a.</li><li>The romanization follows the word divisions of the Devanagari as printed (so 'kāte', 'cana', 'vābhogaratovā', 'bhūtala majinaṃ', 'sajjanasaṃ gatirekā' are run together or split as there); ।, ॥ and the avagraha ऽ are written |, || and '.</li><li>Names: Govinda, Murari (Murāri, 'enemy of Mura') and Vishnu are names of the god Vishnu; Brahman is the absolute; Yama is the god of death; Shankara is the name given in the text itself (13.4, 33.3); the Lord of Shri (śrīpati, 28.2) is Vishnu as the husband of the goddess Śrī.</li><li>line 1.2 (and 34.2): bhajamūḍhamate, 'worship, O you of deluded mind'; mūḍhamati is the one addressed.</li><li>line 1.3: kāle, 'the time', here the time of death.</li><li>line 1.4 (and 33.2): ḍukṛñ karaṇe is a grammarian's entry in the list of verbal roots ('the root kṛ, in the sense of doing'); it stands for grammar rules learnt by rote. Kept in the original words.</li><li>line 3.3: māṃsāvasādi is read as māṃsa-vasā-ādi, 'flesh, fat and the like'.</li><li>line 5.2: stāvan carries over the -s of saktas in line 5.1 by sandhi (saktas tāvan).</li><li>line 5.4: vārtāṃ ... na pṛcchati, literally 'does not ask for news', i.e. no one asks how you are.</li><li>line 6.4: bibhyati, 'fears', is irregular for the expected bibheti.</li><li>saṃsāra (8.2, 10.4, 22.3, 32.2), the round of birth, death and rebirth and the worldly existence it entails, is rendered 'the worldly round'; bhava (14.4, 34.4) 'worldly existence'.</li><li>line 8.4: tattvaṃ cintaya tad iha bhrātaḥ can also be read 'think on that, the truth, here, brother'.</li><li>line 9.4: jīvanmukti, liberation attained while still alive.</li><li>line 10.1: vayasi gate, literally 'when age has gone', i.e. when the prime of life is past.</li><li>line 12.4 (and 16.4, 17.4): āśā is hope, longing or desire; here the longing that keeps one bound to the world.</li><li>line 13.1: mañjarikā, a cluster of blossoms; 'the twelve clusters' names the group of twelve verses ending here.</li><li>line 13.2: vaiyākaraṇasya, genitive, 'of the grammarian': told to him.</li><li>line 13.3: upadeśo bhūd stands for upadeśo 'bhūd ('the teaching was'), the avagraha not written.</li><li>line 13.4: bhagavaccharaṇaiḥ is an honorific plural, 'by the revered Lord'; it can be read as bhagavat + śaraṇaiḥ ('the Lord, the refuge') or as bhagavac-caraṇaiḥ ('the holy feet of the Lord'), both honorifics for the person.</li><li>line 14.1: kā te kāntā-dhana-gata-cintā, 'what is your worry concerning wife and wealth?'; kāntā may be 'wife' or 'beloved'.</li><li>line 15.3: cana is read ca na, 'and not'.</li><li>line 18.1: gaṅgāsāgara, Gangasagar, where the Ganges reaches the sea, a place of pilgrimage; it can also be read 'to the Ganges and to the sea'.</li><li>line 18.3: sarvamatena, 'by the opinion of all', i.e. as all schools agree.</li><li>line 19.2: ajina is the skin of an antelope or deer, worn by ascetics.</li><li>line 21.4: na carcā, 'no discussion, no questioning': Yama does not call him to account.</li><li>line 22.4: apāre, 'shoreless', belongs with saṃsāre in line 22.3.</li><li>line 24.1: āyātaḥ has no stated subject; 'one' is supplied.</li><li>line 24.4: svapna-vicāram, 'a dream-thought': the world seen as a figment of a dream.</li><li>line 26.2: vigraha-sandhi, quarrel and reconciliation (also war and peace).</li><li>line 27.2: ātmānaṃ bhāvaya ko 'ham can also be read 'reflect within yourself: who am I?'</li><li>line 28.1: gītā, the Bhagavad Gita; nāma sahasram, 'the thousand names', of Vishnu.</li><li>line 29.1: sukhataḥ can mean 'for pleasure' or 'readily'; rāmā is a woman.</li><li>line 29.3: maraṇaṃ śaraṇaṃ, 'death is the refuge', i.e. the only end or last resort.</li><li>line 30.1: artham anartham, a play on words: wealth (artha) is non-wealth, ruin (anartha).</li><li>lines 31.1-3: prāṇāyāma (control of the breath), pratyāhāra (withdrawal of the senses) and samādhi (meditative absorption) are terms of yoga; jāpya is the repeated recitation of prayers or names.</li><li>line 33.4: śodhitakaraṇaḥ, 'whose karaṇas (faculties, instruments of knowing) were purified'; karaṇa also echoes 'ḍukṛñ karaṇe' in 33.2.</li></ul></details></section>
